  "question": {
    "text": "An ammeter, voltmeter and a resistor are connected in series to a cell and the readings are noted as <math><mi>I</mi></math> and <math><mi>V</mi></math>. If another resistor <math><mi>R</mi></math> is connected in parallel with a voltmeter, then"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>I</mi></math>  and  <math><mi>V</mi></math>  increase",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>I</mi></math> increases",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>I</mi></math>  and  <math><mi>V</mi></math>  will remain same",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math><mi>I</mi></math>  decreases",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "When the resistor <math><mi>R</mi></math> is connected in parallel with voltmeter, the effective resistance in the circuit decreases because <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math><br /><br />Therefore current, <math><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> increases.<br/><br/>As <math><mi>I</mi></math> increases, potential across <math><mi>R</mi></math> increases and hence potential across voltmeter decreases. Hence reading of voltmeter decreases."
  }
}